= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The direction <vectors> are $\mathbf u=(1,-2,1)$ and $\mathbf v=(0,-2,1)$. Their <cross product> is $(0,-1,-2)$, with <norm> $\sqrt5$. A <unit normal> to the plane is therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbf n=\frac{(0,1,2)}{\sqrt5},\quad\text{or its negative}.}
$$
The two intersecting directions span the plane, so a <vector> <orthogonal> to both is a <normal vector> to that plane.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The plane contains $(1,0,1)$ and has <normal vector> $(0,1,2)$, so its equation is $y+2z=2$. In the equation $\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf r=d$ with a <unit normal>, $|d|$ is the perpendicular distance from the origin. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\text{distance}=\frac2{\sqrt5}.}
$$

= 2
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The exponential definition of <hyperbolic sine> gives $e^z-e^{-z}=0$, or $e^{2z}=1$. Taking every branch of the <complex logarithm> gives
$$
\boxed{\sinh z=0\iff z=i\pi k,\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
For <hyperbolic cosine>, multiply $\cosh z+1=0$ by $2e^z$ to obtain $(e^z+1)^2=0$. Thus $e^z=-1$, giving
$$
\boxed{\cosh z+1=0\iff z=i(2k+1)\pi,\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

The <vectors> $(1,1)^{\mathsf T}$ and $(1,-1)^{\mathsf T}$ are <eigenvectors>: multiplication adds or subtracts the two entries in a row. Their <eigenvalues> are therefore
$$
\boxed{\lambda_+=e^\phi+e^{-\phi}=2\cosh\phi,\qquad\lambda_-=e^\phi-e^{-\phi}=2\sinh\phi.}
$$
The second <eigenvalue> may be positive, zero or negative as the real parameter changes.

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

Multiply the <Maclaurin series> $\ln(1+x^2)=x^2-x^4/2+O(x^6)$ and the <geometric series> $(1+x^2)^{-1}=1-x^2+O(x^4)$. The first two nonzero terms are
$$
\boxed{\frac{\ln(1+x^2)}{1+x^2}=x^2-\frac32x^4+O(x^6).}
$$

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

To distinguish the one-variable profile from the resulting two-variable <function>, write $u(x,t)=F(\xi)$ with $\xi=xe^{-t}$. The <chain rule> gives $u_t=-xe^{-t}F'(\xi)$ and $u_x=e^{-t}F'(\xi)$, so $u_t+xu_x=0$. Thus any differentiable profile of $xe^{-t}$ solves the <partial differential equation>.

The <initial condition> selects $F(\xi)=\ln\xi$ on the positive half-line. Hence
$$
\boxed{u(x,t)=\ln(xe^{-t})=\ln x-t,\qquad x>0.}
$$
This satisfies both the <partial differential equation> and the given data at $t=0$.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $r>0$, the <partial derivatives> of radius are $r_x=x/r$, $r_y=y/r$ and $r_z=z/r$. Applying the <chain rule> to the outer <sine> and then the radial profile gives the <Cartesian coordinates> of the <gradient>:
$$
\boxed{\nabla\sin[f(r)]=\frac{f'(r)\cos[f(r)]}{r}(x,y,z).}
$$
At the origin these coordinate formulas require a differentiable radial extension; differentiability of $f$ as a one-variable profile alone does not guarantee it.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <vector field> has only a vertical component, namely $f(r)$. Its <divergence> is therefore its vertical <partial derivative>:
$$
\boxed{\nabla\cdot[\widehat{\mathbf k}f(r)]=\frac{\partial f(r)}{\partial z}=\frac zr f'(r),\qquad r>0.}
$$

= 7
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <integrating factor> for this <linear ordinary differential equation> is $e^x$. Multiplication gives $(e^xy)'=1$, so $e^xy=x+C$. The <initial condition> sets $C=0$, giving
$$
\boxed{y(x)=xe^{-x}.}
$$

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Near the <initial condition> $y=1$, divide by $y^2$ and separate variables: $y^{-2}\,dy=-x^2\,dx$. Integration gives $-1/y=-x^3/3+C$; the initial value fixes $C=-1$. Thus the solution of the <separable differential equation> is
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac1{1+x^3/3}=\frac3{x^3+3},\qquad x>-\sqrt[3]3.}
$$
The stated interval is the maximal connected interval containing zero: the solution diverges at its left endpoint. The constant solution $y=0$ does not satisfy the <initial condition>.

= 8
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <vector field> is radial, while the differential displacement along a centred <circle> is tangential. These directions are <orthogonal>, so the <dot product> vanishes at every point. Hence
$$
\boxed{\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r=0.}
$$

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the outward <unit normal> on the unit <sphere> and the convention that $\theta$ is measured from the positive vertical axis. Then $\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf n=\sin\theta$, while the <spherical coordinates> <area element> is $dS=\sin\theta\,d\theta\,d\phi$. The <flux> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\int_S\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf S=\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^\pi\sin^2\theta\,d\theta\,d\phi=\pi^2.}
$$

= 9
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

The <gradient> components are
$$
u_x=x^2(3-2x^2)e^{-x^2-y^2},\qquad u_y=-2yx^3e^{-x^2-y^2}.
$$
Away from the vertical axis, $x\ne0$, so $u_y=0$ forces $y=0$. The other <stationary point> equation then gives $x^2=3/2$. Therefore the two requested points are
$$
\boxed{\left(\sqrt{\frac32},0\right),\qquad\left(-\sqrt{\frac32},0\right).}
$$
Every point with $x=0$ also has zero <gradient>, but those points are explicitly excluded.

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Normalization of the <probability density function> requires $1=\alpha\int_0^\pi\sin x\,dx=2\alpha$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\alpha=\frac12.}
$$

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <expected value> follows by <integration by parts>:
$$
E[X]=\frac12\int_0^\pi x\sin x\,dx
=\frac12[-x\cos x+\sin x]_0^\pi=\boxed{\frac\pi2.}
$$
The same result follows from symmetry of the <probability density function> about $\pi/2$.

= 11
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $\Delta=[\mathbf a,\mathbf b,\mathbf c]\ne0$. These three <vectors> are linearly independent, so write $\mathbf d=\alpha\mathbf a+\beta\mathbf b+\gamma\mathbf c$. Taking the <dot product> with $\mathbf b\times\mathbf c$ gives $\alpha=[\mathbf b,\mathbf c,\mathbf d]/\Delta$. Likewise, the <dot products> with $\mathbf c\times\mathbf a$ and $\mathbf a\times\mathbf b$ give
$$
\beta=\frac{[\mathbf c,\mathbf a,\mathbf d]}\Delta=-\frac{[\mathbf c,\mathbf d,\mathbf a]}\Delta,
\qquad\gamma=\frac{[\mathbf a,\mathbf b,\mathbf d]}\Delta.
$$
Here the cyclic invariance and sign change under an interchange of the <scalar triple product> fix every sign. Substituting the three coefficients proves
$$
\boxed{\mathbf d=\frac{[\mathbf b,\mathbf c,\mathbf d]}\Delta\mathbf a
-\frac{[\mathbf c,\mathbf d,\mathbf a]}\Delta\mathbf b
+\frac{[\mathbf a,\mathbf b,\mathbf d]}\Delta\mathbf c.}
$$
This is precisely coordinate extraction using the <reciprocal basis> $(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c,\mathbf c\times\mathbf a,\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)/\Delta$.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $\mathbf v=\mathbf r_0-\mathbf a$. The squared distance to a point on the line is $|\mathbf v-\lambda\widehat{\mathbf t}|^2$. Since $|\widehat{\mathbf t}|=1$, completing the square in $\lambda$ gives its minimum at $\lambda=\widehat{\mathbf t}\cdot\mathbf v$, with value $|\mathbf v|^2-(\widehat{\mathbf t}\cdot\mathbf v)^2$. The <cross product> identity $|\widehat{\mathbf t}\times\mathbf v|^2=|\mathbf v|^2-(\widehat{\mathbf t}\cdot\mathbf v)^2$ therefore proves
$$
\boxed{\ell=|\widehat{\mathbf t}\times(\mathbf r_0-\mathbf a)|.}
$$
For the given line, $\widehat{\mathbf t}=(1,1,0)/\sqrt2$, so its squared distance to $(x,y,z)$ is $(x-y)^2/2+z^2$. Substituting the <parabola> gives
$$
\ell^2(\mu)=6\mu^2+(\mu^2-4)^2=(\mu^2-1)^2+15.
$$
The global minimum occurs at $\mu=\pm1$, hence
$$
\boxed{\ell_{\min}=\sqrt{15}.}
$$
The corresponding points on the <parabola> are $\pm(1,1+2\sqrt3,0)+(0,0,-3)$; their nearest points on the line are $\pm(1+\sqrt3,1+\sqrt3,0)$. The connecting <vectors> are <orthogonal> to the line, as the projection calculation requires.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Write $s=\widehat{\mathbf n}\cdot\mathbf x$. Taking the <dot product> of the equation with the <unit vector> $\widehat{\mathbf n}$ gives $2s+s^2=-1$, so $(s+1)^2=0$ and $s=-1$.

Now take the <cross product> with $\widehat{\mathbf n}$. The <vector triple product> gives $\widehat{\mathbf n}\times(\widehat{\mathbf n}\times\mathbf x)=s\widehat{\mathbf n}-\mathbf x=-\widehat{\mathbf n}-\mathbf x$, so
$$
2\widehat{\mathbf n}\times\mathbf x-\widehat{\mathbf n}-\mathbf x=\widehat{\mathbf n}\times\mathbf b.
$$
The original equation, now with $s^2=1$, also gives $\widehat{\mathbf n}\times\mathbf x=\mathbf b-\widehat{\mathbf n}-2\mathbf x$. Substitution eliminates the remaining <cross product> of the unknown and yields
$$
\boxed{\mathbf x=\frac{2\mathbf b-3\widehat{\mathbf n}-\widehat{\mathbf n}\times\mathbf b}{5}.}
$$
Its <dot product> with $\widehat{\mathbf n}$ is $-1$, and direct substitution using the <vector triple product> verifies the original equation. The elimination gives a unique solution without choosing components. It is also an application of the <inverse of a scalar plus a cross-product operator> after the scalar component has been fixed.

= 12
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $r>0$, differentiate $r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ while keeping the other coordinate fixed in the <Cartesian coordinate system>:
$$
\boxed{r_x=\frac xr=\cos\phi,\qquad r_y=\frac yr=\sin\phi.}
$$
The origin is excluded from this <polar coordinates> chart.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Differentiate the angular relation locally, or use $d\phi=(x\,dy-y\,dx)/(x^2+y^2)$. Holding each other coordinate fixed gives
$$
\boxed{\phi_x=-\frac y{r^2}=-\frac{\sin\phi}{r},\qquad
\phi_y=\frac x{r^2}=\frac{\cos\phi}{r}.}
$$
These are local <partial derivatives> on a consistent angular branch, independent of where its branch cut is placed.

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Apply the multivariable <chain rule> to $f(x,y)=g(r(x,y),\phi(x,y))$. Using the coordinate <partial derivatives> above gives
$$
\boxed{f_x=g_r\cos\phi-\frac{g_\phi}{r}\sin\phi,\qquad
f_y=g_r\sin\phi+\frac{g_\phi}{r}\cos\phi.}
$$
Equivalently, $\partial_x=\cos\phi\,\partial_r-(\sin\phi/r)\partial_\phi$ and $\partial_y=\sin\phi\,\partial_r+(\cos\phi/r)\partial_\phi$. Their coefficients vary with position and must also be differentiated when applying them twice.

= d
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Write $c=\cos\phi$ and $s=\sin\phi$. The needed coefficient <derivatives> are $c_x=s^2/r$, $s_x=-sc/r$, $c_y=-sc/r$ and $s_y=c^2/r$. Apply the <product rule> to both terms of $f_x=cg_r-(s/r)g_\phi$ and use the <chain rule> for the <derivatives> of $g$. Collecting terms gives
$$
\boxed{f_{xx}=c^2g_{rr}-\frac{2sc}{r}g_{r\phi}+\frac{2sc}{r^2}g_\phi
+\frac{s^2}{r}g_r+\frac{s^2}{r^2}g_{\phi\phi}.}
$$
Similarly, differentiating $f_y=sg_r+(c/r)g_\phi$ gives
$$
\boxed{f_{yy}=s^2g_{rr}+\frac{2sc}{r}g_{r\phi}-\frac{2sc}{r^2}g_\phi
+\frac{c^2}{r}g_r+\frac{c^2}{r^2}g_{\phi\phi}.}
$$
On addition, both the mixed <partial derivative> terms and the first angular <derivative> terms cancel. Using $s^2+c^2=1$, the <Laplacian> in <polar coordinates> is
$$
\boxed{\nabla^2f=g_{rr}+\frac1r g_r+\frac1{r^2}g_{\phi\phi},\qquad r>0.}
$$

= e
{parent=12}
{scope}

= i
{parent=e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <double-angle formula> gives $r^2\sin2\phi=2(r\cos\phi)(r\sin\phi)=2xy$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{f(x,y)=2xy,\qquad\nabla f=(2y,2x).}
$$

= ii
{parent=e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

In the positive quadrant, both components of the <gradient> are positive. Along $y=x$ the arrows point along $(1,1)$; near the horizontal axis they point mainly upwards, and near the vertical axis mainly rightwards. The <level sets> are the <hyperbolas> $xy=f/2$, including the coordinate axes for level zero. The <gradient> is <orthogonal> to each regular <level set> and points towards increasing values of $f$.

\Image[paper-2-2018-q12-gradient-contours.png]
{title=Gradient vectors and hyperbolic level sets of twice the coordinate product}

= iii
{parent=e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

At $(1,2)$, the <gradient> is $(4,2)$. The requested direction must be normalized to the <unit vector> $(1,1)/\sqrt2$. The <directional derivative> is therefore
$$
\boxed{D_{(1,1)/\sqrt2}f(1,2)=\nabla f(1,2)\cdot\frac{(1,1)}{\sqrt2}=3\sqrt2.}
$$
Using $(1,1)$ without normalization would instead measure change per unit of a differently scaled path parameter.

= 13
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The field $\mathbf F$ is not a <conservative vector field>: either nonzero closed <line integral> computed below rules out a <scalar potential>. Equivalently, its planar <curl> is $\partial_xF_y-\partial_yF_x=1-(-1)=2$.

For $\mathbf G$, integrating $\Phi_x=2xy^2$ gives $\Phi=x^2y^2+h(y)$. Matching $\Phi_y=2yx^2$ then gives $h'=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathbf F\text{ is not conservative};\qquad\mathbf G=\nabla\Phi,\quad\Phi=x^2y^2+C.}
$$
This also explains why both closed <line integrals> of $\mathbf G$ vanish.

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Starting at $(1,0)$, traverse the right, top, left and bottom sides in that order. For $\mathbf F$, the differential expression is $-y\,dx+x\,dy$. The four <line integral> contributions are respectively $1,1,0,0$, so the total is two.

For $\mathbf G$, the expression is $2xy^2\,dx+2yx^2\,dy$. The right side contributes $\int_0^12y\,dy=1$, and the top side contributes $\int_1^02x\,dx=-1$; the other sides contribute zero. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\oint_{\rm square}\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r=2,\qquad
\oint_{\rm square}\mathbf G\cdot d\mathbf r=0.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Parametrize the <circle> by $\mathbf r(t)=(\cos t,\sin t)$, $0\le t\le2\pi$. Its tangent is $\mathbf r'=(-\sin t,\cos t)=\mathbf F(\mathbf r(t))$, so the <dot product> $\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf r'$ is one. For the other field,
$$
\mathbf G\cdot\mathbf r'=2\sin t\cos t(\cos^2t-\sin^2t)=\frac12\sin4t.
$$
Integrating around the full <circle> gives
$$
\boxed{\oint_{\rm circle}\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r=2\pi,\qquad
\oint_{\rm circle}\mathbf G\cdot d\mathbf r=0.}
$$

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Substituting the endpoint parameter values gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbf r(0)=(1,0,0),\qquad\mathbf r(\pi/12)=(0,1,2\pi/3).}
$$
The curve lies on the unit circular <cylinder> $x^2+y^2=1$. Its azimuth increases from zero to $\pi/2$ while its height increases linearly, so it is one quarter-turn of a rising <helix>. The arrow in the sketch indicates increasing parameter.

\Image[paper-2-2018-q13-quarter-helix.png]
{title=Quarter-turn helix with its endpoints and projection onto the horizontal plane}

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <arc length> factor is the <norm> of the tangent:
$$
\left|\frac{d\mathbf r}{dt}\right|=\sqrt{36\sin^26t+36\cos^26t+64}=10.
$$
Hence $ds=10\,dt$ and $\Phi=xy=\cos6t\sin6t=\tfrac12\sin12t$. The scalar <line integral> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\int_\Gamma\Phi\,ds=5\int_0^{\pi/12}\sin12t\,dt=\frac56.}
$$
This uses scalar <arc length>, rather than the oriented displacement used in the vector <line integrals> of part (a).

= 14
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Distinguish the production type as well as the stopping position. The <sample space> has the six outcomes
$$
\boxed{\Omega=\{G\cap x_0,G\cap x_1,G\cap x_2,D\cap x_0,D\cap x_1,D\cap x_2\}.}
$$
Here $x_k$ in an event means $X=x_k$. Their joint <probabilities> are $pg_k$ and $(1-p)d_k$, respectively. The conditional stopping laws obey $\sum_{k=0}^2g_k=\sum_{k=0}^2d_k=1$; with the stated strict positivity all six outcomes are possible.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Multiply the production <probability> by the conditional stopping <probability>:
$$
\boxed{P(G\cap x_0)=P(G)P(x_0\mid G)=pg_0.}
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The good and defective cases are disjoint and exhaustive. The <law of total probability> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{P(x_0)=pg_0+(1-p)d_0.}
$$

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Divide the relevant joint <probability> by the marginal stopping <probability>. By <Bayes' theorem>,
$$
\boxed{P(D\mid x_1)=\frac{(1-p)d_1}{pg_1+(1-p)d_1}.}
$$
The denominator is positive under the stated assumptions.

= v
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

The condition $X>x_0$ selects positions $x_1$ and $x_2$. Their combined good-ball joint <probability> is $p(g_1+g_2)$, and their total <probability> is $p(g_1+g_2)+(1-p)(d_1+d_2)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{P(G\mid X>x_0)=\frac{p(g_1+g_2)}{p(g_1+g_2)+(1-p)(d_1+d_2)}
=\frac{p(1-g_0)}{p(1-g_0)+(1-p)(1-d_0)}.}
$$

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For $0\le k\le n$, sum the marginal stopping <probabilities> over the selected tail:
$$
\boxed{P(X>x_k)=p\sum_{j=k+1}^ng_j+(1-p)\sum_{j=k+1}^nd_j.}
$$
Equivalently it is $p(1-\sum_{j=0}^kg_j)+(1-p)(1-\sum_{j=0}^kd_j)$. At $k=n$, both empty tail sums are zero, as required.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The event includes stopping positions zero through $k$. Applying <Bayes' theorem> to their joint and marginal <probabilities> gives
$$
\boxed{P(G\mid X\le x_k)=\frac{p\sum_{j=0}^kg_j}{p\sum_{j=0}^kg_j+(1-p)\sum_{j=0}^kd_j}.}
$$
This <conditional probability> is defined whenever its conditioning event has positive <probability>. At $k=n$, it reduces to $p$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

There are $99$ possible stopping points, so the equal defective-ball <probabilities> are $d_k=1/99$. With $g_0>0$, the desired <conditional probability> gives
$$
\frac{pg_0}{pg_0+(1-p)/99}=\frac{99}{100},
\qquad100pg_0=99pg_0+1-p.
$$
Thus, for a fixed good-ball stopping law,
$$
\boxed{p=\frac1{1+g_0}.}
$$
Since $g_0\le1$, the smallest possible production <probability> over admissible stopping laws is
$$
\boxed{p_{\min}=\frac12,\qquad\text{attained when }g_0=1.}
$$
If strict positivity of every $g_k$ is additionally imposed in this general case, then $g_0<1$ and one only has the infimum $1/2$, approached as $g_0\to1$. The paper states that strict-positivity assumption explicitly for part (a), not for the general case in part (b). Without choosing or bounding $g_0$, the exact fixed-law answer remains $1/(1+g_0)$.

= 15
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the <homogeneous linear differential equation>, the <characteristic equation> is $r^2-2r+2=0$, with <polynomial roots> $1\pm i$. Its <homogeneous solution> is therefore $e^x(A\cos x+B\sin x)$.

Use the <method of undetermined coefficients> for a <particular solution> $y_p=ax^2+bx+c$. Substitution gives
$$
2ax^2+(2b-4a)x+(2a-2b+2c)=2x^2.
$$
Equating coefficients yields $a=1$, $b=2$ and $c=1$. Hence the <general solution> is
$$
\boxed{y=e^x(A\cos x+B\sin x)+x^2+2x+1.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <characteristic polynomial> factors as $(r+1)(r+2)$, so the <homogeneous solution> is $Ae^{-x}+Be^{-2x}$. The forcing $e^{-x}$ already occurs in that solution, so a constant multiple of $e^{-x}$ cannot be a <particular solution>. Use $y_p=Cxe^{-x}$ instead. Its <derivatives> are $C(1-x)e^{-x}$ and $C(x-2)e^{-x}$, so substitution gives $Ce^{-x}$ and fixes $C=1$. Thus
$$
\boxed{y=Ae^{-x}+Be^{-2x}+xe^{-x}.}
$$
The extra factor of $x$ accounts for the repeated forcing exponent in the <method of undetermined coefficients>.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Under $y=xu$, the <product rule> gives $y'=u+xu'$ and $y''=2u'+xu''$. Substitute these into the original equation:
$$
x(2u'+xu'')-(u+xu')+u=x^2u''+xu'=x(xu''+u')=0.
$$
Since $x>0$, this is precisely $xu''+u'=0$, or $(xu')'=0$. Integrating twice gives $xu'=C_1$ and $u=C_1\ln x+C_2$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{y(x)=x(C_1\ln x+C_2),\qquad x>0.}
$$
The two independent <solutions of a differential equation> supply the <general solution> of this second-order <linear ordinary differential equation>.

= 16
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The fixed <vectors> have constant components, so $\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b$ is constant, $\nabla\cdot\mathbf a=0$ and $\nabla\cdot\mathbf r=3$. Hence the <divergence> is
$$
\boxed{\nabla\cdot[2(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b)\mathbf r+\mathbf a]=6\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <curl> of the constant <vector field> $\mathbf a\times\mathbf b$ vanishes. The <curl> of the position field $\mathbf r=(x,y,z)$ also vanishes, since all its cross-coordinate <partial derivatives> are zero. Thus
$$
\boxed{\nabla\times[(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)+\mathbf r]=\mathbf0.}
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <vector triple product> rewrites the field as $\mathbf b(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf r)-\mathbf r(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b)$. Its first term has <divergence> $\mathbf b\cdot\nabla(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf r)=\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b$, while the second contributes $3\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\nabla\cdot[\mathbf a\times(\mathbf b\times\mathbf r)]=-2\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b.}
$$

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Again write the field as $\mathbf b(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf r)-\mathbf r(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b)$. The second term has zero <curl>. Applying the <product rule> to the first gives $\nabla(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf r)\times\mathbf b=\mathbf a\times\mathbf b$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\nabla\times[\mathbf a\times(\mathbf b\times\mathbf r)]=\mathbf a\times\mathbf b.}
$$

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the orientation induced by the boundary order $O\to A\to B\to O$. The triangle's <vector area element> integrates to $\tfrac12\mathbf a\times\mathbf b$. Every point $\mathbf r$ in its plane is a linear combination of $\mathbf a,\mathbf b$, so $\mathbf r\cdot(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)=0$. The radial term of the field therefore contributes no <flux>. The constant term gives
$$
\boxed{\int_{OAB}\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf S
=(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)\cdot\frac{\mathbf a\times\mathbf b}{2}
=\frac12|\mathbf a\times\mathbf b|^2.}
$$
Reversing the orientation reverses this sign. If $\mathbf a$ and $\mathbf b$ are collinear, the triangle has zero <area> and the displayed value is zero by the degenerate-area convention.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Take the outward orientation of the complete closed surface, including its flat base. The constant part of the <vector field> has zero <divergence>, and the radial part has <divergence> $3\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b$. The <divergence theorem> gives its <flux> as that constant times the solid hemisphere's <volume>, $2\pi R^3/3$:
$$
\boxed{\int_{\partial V}\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf S=3(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b)\frac{2\pi R^3}{3}
=2\pi R^3\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b.}
$$
The constant field may have nonzero <flux> through the curved part alone, but its curved and base contributions cancel on the closed surface.

= 17
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Reading off coefficients gives the <matrix> equation
$$
\boxed{A\mathbf r=\mathbf b,\qquad
A=\begin{pmatrix}1&\mu&0\\1&-1&3\\2&-2&\mu\end{pmatrix},\quad
\mathbf r=\begin{pmatrix}x\\y\\z\end{pmatrix},\quad
\mathbf b=\begin{pmatrix}b_1\\b_2\\b_3\end{pmatrix}.}
$$

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Multiplication of a <matrix> by a column <vector> forms the linear combination of its columns with coefficients from that <vector>. Here
$$
\boxed{\mathbf c_1=(1,1,2)^{\mathsf T},\quad\mathbf c_2=(\mu,-1,-2)^{\mathsf T},\quad\mathbf c_3=(0,3,\mu)^{\mathsf T},}
$$
so $A\mathbf r=x\mathbf c_1+y\mathbf c_2+z\mathbf c_3$. Equating this expression with $\mathbf b$ reproduces the original three <linear equations>.

= c
{parent=17}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For $\mathbf b=0$, a nonzero coefficient <vector> $(x,y,z)$ with $x\mathbf c_1+y\mathbf c_2+z\mathbf c_3=0$ is precisely a linear dependence of the three columns. Three <vectors> in three dimensions are linearly dependent exactly when the <determinant> of their column <matrix>, or their <scalar triple product>, is zero. Thus
$$
\boxed{\text{a nontrivial solution requires }[\mathbf c_1,\mathbf c_2,\mathbf c_3]=0.}
$$
For this square homogeneous <linear system>, the condition is also sufficient.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Subtract twice the second row from the third row when computing the <determinant>. The last row becomes $(0,0,\mu-6)$, so
$$
\det A=(\mu-6)\det\begin{pmatrix}1&\mu\\1&-1\end{pmatrix}=-(\mu-6)(\mu+1).
$$
Its zeros give exactly the parameter values with a nontrivial <null space>:
$$
\boxed{\mu=-1\quad\text{or}\quad\mu=6.}
$$

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

At $\mu=-1$, the first equation gives $x=y$, and the second then gives $z=0$. All nonzero solutions are
$$
\boxed{\mathbf r=t(1,1,0)^{\mathsf T},\qquad t\ne0\quad(\mu=-1).}
$$
At $\mu=6$, the first equation gives $x=-6y$. The second then gives $3z=7y$, and the third is twice the second. Choosing an integer direction <vector>, all nonzero solutions are
$$
\boxed{\mathbf r=t(-18,3,7)^{\mathsf T},\qquad t\ne0\quad(\mu=6).}
$$
Each full <null space>, including the zero solution, is a line through the origin. Geometrically, the three homogeneous constraint planes have that common intersection; in each case only two of their equations are independent.

= d
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

At $\mu=-1$, $\mathbf c_2=-\mathbf c_1$, while $\mathbf c_1$ and $\mathbf c_3$ are independent. Their span is a plane. A solution requires $\mathbf b$ to lie in that plane, so every <scalar triple product> formed from two columns and $\mathbf b$ must vanish. The product with $\mathbf c_1,\mathbf c_2$ vanishes identically; the other two give the plane condition.

Explicitly, $\mathbf c_1\times\mathbf c_3=(-7,1,3)$, so
$$
[\mathbf c_1,\mathbf c_3,\mathbf b]=(-7,1,3)\cdot(1,\lambda,0)=\lambda-7.
$$
Thus $\lambda=7$ is necessary and, since the two columns span the plane, sufficient. The equations then give $x-y=1$ and $z=2$, so
$$
\boxed{\lambda=7,\qquad\mathbf r=(1,0,2)^{\mathsf T}+t(1,1,0)^{\mathsf T},\quad t\in\mathbb R.}
$$
The solution is an affine line parallel to the homogeneous <null space> from part (c), obtained by intersecting the compatible constraint planes. Every point on it is nonzero because $z=2$.

= 18
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

With the real <Fourier series> convention,
$$
\boxed{f(\theta)\sim\frac{a_0}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^\infty[a_n\cos(n\theta)+b_n\sin(n\theta)],}
$$
where the <Fourier coefficients> are
$$
\boxed{a_n=\frac1\pi\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(t)\cos(nt)\,dt\quad(n\ge0),\qquad
b_n=\frac1\pi\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(t)\sin(nt)\,dt\quad(n\ge1).}
$$
For a square-integrable periodic <function>, <Parseval's identity> is
$$
\boxed{\frac1\pi\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}|f(t)|^2\,dt=\frac{|a_0|^2}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^\infty(|a_n|^2+|b_n|^2).}
$$
The <Fourier coefficients> require integrability, and the stated <Parseval identity> requires square integrability. The expansion agrees with $f$ in the <L2 space> sense; for piecewise smooth data it converges pointwise to the average of the two one-sided limits. Without such qualifications an arbitrary periodic <function> need not have a pointwise-convergent <Fourier series>.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For fixed $r<1$, $\sum r^n\cos(n\theta)$ converges absolutely and uniformly. Thus for integrable $f$, one may integrate the kernel <series> term by term. The <cosine addition formula> gives
$$
\cos[n(\theta-t)]=\cos(n\theta)\cos(nt)+\sin(n\theta)\sin(nt).
$$
Substitute this into the defining <integral>. The constant kernel term gives $\tfrac1{2\pi}\int f=a_0/2$; the remaining terms give
$$
\boxed{y_f(r,\theta)=\frac{a_0}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^\infty r^n[a_n\cos(n\theta)+b_n\sin(n\theta)],}
$$
where
$$
\boxed{a_n=\frac1\pi\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(t)\cos(nt)\,dt,\qquad
b_n=\frac1\pi\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(t)\sin(nt)\,dt.}
$$
These are the same <Fourier coefficients> as in part (a). The kernel $2K$ is the <Poisson kernel for the unit disk>; the factors $r^n$ suppress the higher angular frequencies inside the <disk>.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The boundary data are odd except at isolated endpoint choices, which do not change <integrals>. Thus every cosine <Fourier coefficient>, including $a_0$, vanishes. For the sine <Fourier coefficients>,
$$
b_n=\frac2\pi\int_0^\pi\sin(nt)\,dt=\frac{2[1-(-1)^n]}{\pi n}
=\begin{cases}4/(\pi n),&n\text{ odd},\\0,&n\text{ even}.\end{cases}
$$
Consequently the extension is
$$
\boxed{y_{f_0}(r,\theta)=\frac4\pi\sum_{\substack{n\ge1\\n\text{ odd}}}\frac{r^n\sin(n\theta)}n.}
$$
It converges absolutely for every $r<1$; its limiting boundary values at jumps are the midpoint of the two one-sided limits.

= d
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

At a fixed radius, <Parseval's identity> applied to the preceding <Fourier series> gives
$$
\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}[y_{f_0}(r,\theta)]^2\,d\theta
=\pi\sum_{\substack{n\ge1\\n\text{ odd}}}\frac{16r^{2n}}{\pi^2n^2}
=\frac{16}{\pi}\sum_{\substack{n\ge1\\n\text{ odd}}}\frac{r^{2n}}{n^2}.
$$
In <polar coordinates>, $dA=r\,dr\,d\theta$. Integrating over radius and interchanging the nonnegative sum and <integral> gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_D[y_{f_0}]^2\,dA
&=\frac{16}{\pi}\sum_{\substack{n\ge1\\n\text{ odd}}}\frac1{n^2}\int_0^1r^{2n+1}\,dr\\
&=\boxed{\frac8\pi\sum_{\substack{n\ge1\\n\text{ odd}}}\frac1{n^2(n+1)},\qquad p=2.}
\end{aligned}
$$
The interchange follows from the <monotone convergence theorem>. This is the special case of the <area norm of the Poisson extension in the unit disk> for the odd step boundary data.

= 19
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=19}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $h=x^2+y^2+z-x$. Using <Lagrange multipliers> with $\nabla f=\lambda\nabla h$ gives
$$
ay=\lambda(2x-1),\qquad ax=2\lambda y,\qquad1=\lambda.
$$
The third equation fixes $\lambda=1$. The first two then reduce to $2x-ay=1$ and $2y=ax$. If $a^2\ne4$, their solution and the constraint give
$$
\boxed{x=\frac2{4-a^2},\qquad y=\frac a{4-a^2},\qquad
z=\frac{4-3a^2}{(4-a^2)^2}.}
$$
At $a=2$, the second equation requires $y=x$ and makes the first inconsistent; at $a=-2$, it requires $y=-x$ and gives the same inconsistency. Thus **there is no constrained stationary point when $a=\pm2$**. The constraint <gradient> never vanishes because its vertical component is one, so the multiplier condition has not excluded any regular constrained <stationary points>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Eliminate $z$ using the constraint: $z=x-x^2-y^2$. The restricted objective is the <quadratic polynomial> $F(x,y)=axy+x-x^2-y^2$, with constant <Hessian matrix>
$$
H=\begin{pmatrix}-2&a\\a&-2\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Its <eigenvalues> are $-2+a$ and $-2-a$. Hence
$$
\boxed{|a|<2:\text{ strict constrained maximum};\qquad |a|>2:\text{ constrained saddle}.}
$$
For $|a|<2$, both <eigenvalues> are negative and the <quadratic polynomial> is strictly concave, so the maximum is global on the entire constraint surface. For $|a|>2$, the opposite signs give increasing and decreasing directions. At $a=\pm2$ there is no <stationary point>, as already found, rather than a degenerate extremum to classify. No parameter value produces a constrained <local minimum>.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $\epsilon_k=E_0(k+\tfrac12)$ and use <Lagrange multipliers> $\alpha,\beta$ for the particle-number and energy constraints. The stationary equations are
$$
\frac{\partial}{\partial n_k}\left[-\sum_j(n_j\ln n_j-n_j)-\alpha\left(\sum_jn_j-N\right)-\beta\left(\sum_j\epsilon_jn_j-E\right)\right]
=-\ln n_k-\alpha-\beta\epsilon_k=0.
$$
Thus $n_k=e^{-\alpha}e^{-\beta\epsilon_k}$. Finite normalization over infinitely many levels requires $\beta>0$. Put $q=e^{-\beta E_0}$, so $0<q<1$. The <geometric series> gives
$$
N=e^{-\alpha}\frac{e^{-\beta E_0/2}}{1-q},\qquad
e^{-\alpha}=N(1-q)e^{\beta E_0/2}=2N\sinh\frac{\beta E_0}{2}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{n_k=2N\sinh\left(\frac{\beta E_0}{2}\right)e^{-\beta E_0(k+1/2)}.}
$$
For the energy constraint, use $n_k=N(1-q)q^k$ and $\sum kq^k=q/(1-q)^2$, obtained by differentiating the <geometric series>. This yields
$$
\frac E{NE_0}=(1-q)\sum_{k=0}^\infty\left(k+\frac12\right)q^k
=\frac12+\frac q{1-q}=\frac{1+q}{2(1-q)}.
$$
Recognizing the <hyperbolic cotangent> gives
$$
\boxed{E=\frac{NE_0}{2}\coth\left(\frac{\beta E_0}{2}\right).}
$$
The strict positivity of all occupations requires $E>NE_0/2$; the paper's positivity assumptions alone do not guarantee this feasibility condition. For admissible energy the multiplier is uniquely $\beta=E_0^{-1}\ln[(2E+NE_0)/(2E-NE_0)]>0$. Equality $E=NE_0/2$ is only the limiting state with all occupation in level zero, outside the stipulated strictly positive family; lower energy is impossible.

For rigor beyond formal multiplier equations, $p_k=n_k/N$ has fixed mean $E/(NE_0)-1/2$. The objective differs from $N$ times its <information entropy> only by the fixed constant $N-N\ln N$. The <maximum entropy distribution on the nonnegative integers> is uniquely geometric at that mean, confirming the stationary family and its maximum character.

= 20
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <chain rule> for the new coordinates gives $\partial_x=\partial_\xi+\partial_\eta$ and $\partial_y=\partial_\xi-\partial_\eta$. Since the coordinates depend linearly on $x,y$, their coefficients are constant. Expanding the squares of these differential operators gives
$$
u_{xx}-u_{yy}=(u_{\xi\xi}+2u_{\xi\eta}+u_{\eta\eta})-(u_{\xi\xi}-2u_{\xi\eta}+u_{\eta\eta})=4u_{\xi\eta}.
$$
Hence the <partial differential equation> becomes $u_{\xi\eta}=1/4$. Integrating once in each variable introduces independent one-variable functions, giving the <general solution>
$$
\boxed{u=\frac{\xi\eta}{4}+F(\xi)+G(\eta)
=\frac{x^2-y^2}{4}+F(x+y)+G(x-y),}
$$
where $F,G$ are arbitrary twice-differentiable functions on the relevant characteristic intervals. Their sum includes an arbitrary additive constant.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The first <boundary condition> requires $u(x,0)=ax^2+d=0$ for all $x$, so $a=d=0$. Since $u_y=bx+2cy$, the second <boundary condition> gives $b=0$. The <partial differential equation> then reads $2a-2c=1$, fixing $c=-1/2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{a=0,\quad b=0,\quad c=-\frac12,\quad d=0,\qquad u(x,y)=-\frac{y^2}{2}.}
$$

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

On a smooth implicit branch, put $Q=x+z^2$ and require $Q\ne0$. This is exactly the <implicit function theorem> condition, since the defining equation has vertical <partial derivative> $3Q$. Differentiating the cubic relation in $x$ and $y$ gives
$$
Qz_x=-z,\qquad Qz_y=1,
\qquad z_x=-\frac zQ,\quad z_y=\frac1Q.
$$
For a second <derivative>, the dependence of $Q$ on $z$ must also be included: $Q_x=1+2zz_x$ and $Q_y=2zz_y$. Thus
$$
\begin{aligned}
z_{xx}&=-\frac{z_x}{Q}+\frac{z(1+2zz_x)}{Q^2}=\frac{2xz}{Q^3},\\
z_{yy}&=-\frac{2zz_y}{Q^2}=-\frac{2z}{Q^3},\\
z_{xy}&=-\frac{z_y}{Q}+\frac{2z^2z_y}{Q^2}=\frac{z^2-x}{Q^3}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The required cancellation follows immediately:
$$
\boxed{z_{xx}+xz_{yy}=\frac{2xz}{(x+z^2)^3}-\frac{2xz}{(x+z^2)^3}=0.}
$$
The verification is local to smooth branches with $x+z^2\ne0$; the implicit relation does not guarantee a differentiable function at its singular points.
